{
  "summary": "One finding needs your attention soon: a scheduled task named \\TelemetryRefresh downloads text from the internet and runs it straight away as commands. That is a pattern malware often uses, so look at it first. The other finding is a routine check that you recognize an extra administrator account, which is often perfectly normal.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Scheduled task that downloads and runs commands from the internet",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-SUSPICIOUS:2041796b"
      ],
      "explanation": "A scheduled task called \\TelemetryRefresh is set up to run automatically under your account (<user>). It fetches a file from a web address (cdn.example.invalid) and feeds whatever it contains directly into the Windows command prompt. Whoever controls that web address could make your computer run any command, and you would not see it happen. The name sounds like a normal Windows feature, but the command itself is the concern. The task runs with limited rights, not full administrator rights, which reduces the damage it can do but does not make it safe. If you did not create this task yourself or knowingly install software that did, treat it as suspicious. See the fix shown for this finding in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Confirm you recognize an extra administrator account",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The computer has a local account, <computer>\\<account-2>, with administrator rights, which means it can change anything on the computer. Having a separate admin account is often good practice, so this may be completely expected. Just confirm it is an account you or someone you trust set up. If you don't recognize it, especially given the suspicious task above, take it more seriously. The report shows what to do."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
